      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from red stones on the riverbank that prospectors mistook for rubies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tananachiefs.org/about/communities/ruby/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ruby sits on the south bank of the Yukon River, at the edge of the Nowitna National Wildlife Refuge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Access to Ruby is limited to boat or air, reflecting its remote Alaskan location.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1998, the largest gold nugget ever found in Alaska, weighing 294.10 troy ounces, was discovered near Ruby.",
